# .env.example — copy to .env and fill in for your deployment (#96).
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored (your instance-specific values); this template is committed.
# NEVER put real secrets/keys in .env.example — only here-are-the-variables placeholders.
#
# Two kinds of variables live here:
#   1. docker-compose substitution vars — read from .env by `docker compose` (see docker-compose.yml)
#   2. YOURPHR_* app config overrides — read by the backend (prefix YOURPHR; '.'/'-' become '_'),
#      e.g. config key `jwt.issuer.key` -> env `YOURPHR_JWT_ISSUER_KEY`.
# Layering (lowest -> highest precedence): built-in defaults < .env (this committed base, after
# cp/fill) < .env_custom (gitignored, per-deployment overrides) < real environment (YOURPHR_* vars).
# Put instance overrides in .env_custom to keep them out of git.
#
# WHERE these files are read from: at startup the backend loads `.env_custom` then `.env` from its
# WORKING DIRECTORY (relative paths), so:
#   - local dev (`make serve-backend`, run from the repo root) -> keep .env / .env_custom at repo root;
#   - Docker / k8s (container WORKDIR is /opt/fasten/) -> mount .env_custom at /opt/fasten/.env_custom.
# Both files are OPTIONAL: config works on defaults + YOURPHR_* env alone. The live (flux) deployment
# sets YOURPHR_* env vars directly and uses no dotenv file.
#
# DOCKER COMPOSE — IMPORTANT (#397): `docker compose` reads THIS file only to substitute ${...}
# inside docker-compose.yml. It does NOT forward these values into the container. The compose files
# therefore declare `env_file:` so YOURPHR_* settings here actually reach the app. If you are using a
# docker-compose.yml from before v1.13.4 it lacks that block, and everything below is silently
# ignored — no warning, the app just runs with defaults. Verify with:
#
#   docker compose config | grep YOURPHR_
#
# If your variables are not listed there, the app will not see them.

# --- docker-compose (docker-compose.yml) ---
# Container hostname (appears in logs / the app footer).
HOSTNAME=fasten
# Host bind IP and published port. PORT maps to the container's :8080.
IP=0.0.0.0
PORT=9090

# --- deployment / reverse proxy (instance-specific) ---
# Your domain when fronting the app with a reverse proxy (e.g. Traefik/nginx + forward-auth).
domainname=example.com

# --- app config overrides (YOURPHR_*; override config.yaml) ---
# SECURITY: pin your own JWT signing key instead of the public default placeholder.
#   generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# Leave UNSET to auto-generate + persist a strong key at <db-dir>/.jwt_issuer_key (#102).
# YOURPHR_JWT_ISSUER_KEY=

# SQLite DB encryption key (DB encryption is on by default).
# YOURPHR_DATABASE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Instance data root (#451) — ONE top folder holding everything this instance owns and must
# not lose. Set this and the rest derive under it:
#
#   <data root>/db/fasten.db     the SQLite database
#   <data root>/cache/           dashboard cache
#   <data root>/backups/         backups
#   <data root>/.jwt_issuer_key  auto-generated signing key
#   <data root>/...              instance settings files
#
# This is the single directory to mount as a volume and to back up. Leave UNSET to keep the
# legacy behaviour, where the root is derived from YOURPHR_DATABASE_LOCATION's parent — which
# is what every consumer did before this key existed, so upgrading installs do not move.
# YOURPHR_STORAGE_DATA_DIR=/opt/fasten/data

# Where the SQLite DB lives (inside the container). Setting this explicitly OVERRIDES the
# derived <data root>/db/fasten.db — useful to keep the DB on fast local disk while the rest
# of the data root lives elsewhere. Leave unset to let it follow the data root.
# YOURPHR_DATABASE_LOCATION=/opt/fasten/db/fasten.db

# Backend listen port inside the container (default 8080).
# YOURPHR_WEB_LISTEN_PORT=8080

# Deployment label shown in the UI footer as "<name>-<semver>" (e.g. demo-1.18.2).
# Same release image for every instance — set per env so demo is not labeled "prod".
# YOURPHR_WEB_ENVIRONMENT_NAME=demo

# App log level: DEBUG | INFO | WARN | ERROR
# YOURPHR_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- C-CDA / CCD (XML) import (#254, #397, #404) ---
# Provider portals (Epic MyChart and friends) often export XML C-CDA documents rather than FHIR
# JSON. YourPHR converts those to FHIR using a separate, stateless sidecar container.
#
# Since v1.15.0 (#404) this works OUT OF THE BOX: the shipped compose files start the converter
# automatically and config.yaml points the app at it. `docker compose up -d` is all you need.
#
# The variables below are OVERRIDES, not requirements:
#   - turn the feature OFF if you only import FHIR JSON and would rather not run the extra
#     container (it is stateless and stores nothing, so removing it loses no data);
#   - point at a converter elsewhere, e.g. a bare k8s Deployment where the shipped compose file
#     is not in play (see deploy/yourphr-cda-converter.example.yaml).
#
# NOTE the YOURPHR_ prefix. The config KEYS are cda_converter.enabled / cda_converter.url; the
# ENV VARS are the keys upper-cased with the prefix. FASTEN_* and unprefixed names are ignored.
# YOURPHR_CDA_CONVERTER_ENABLED=false
# The sidecar is internal-only (raw C-CDA is PHI) — reach it on the compose network by service name.
# YOURPHR_CDA_CONVERTER_URL=http://cda-converter:8080
# Conversion timeout for a single document; raise it for very large exports.
# YOURPHR_CDA_CONVERTER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60

# --- Epic SMART on FHIR sandbox (reference only — NOT consumed as env vars) ---
# YourPHR uses bring-your-own client_id: these values are entered in the
# "Connect a SMART source" UI (the "Use Epic Sandbox" button pre-fills them),
# not read from the environment. Listed here as a copy-paste reference.
# Full walkthrough: docs/vendors/epic-sandbox.md
#
#   FHIR base URL : https://fhir.epic.com/interconnect-fhir-oauth/api/FHIR/R4
#   Scopes        : launch/patient patient/*.read openid fhirUser offline_access
#   Redirect URI  : <your relay>/callback   (register this with Epic — see YOURPHR_RELAY_* below)
#   Client ID     : your own Non-Production Client ID from https://fhir.epic.com (BYO)

# --- SMART on FHIR OAuth relay (#50, #399) ---
# The relay is fully runtime-configurable — no rebuild needed to point at your own.
# The exact callback URL your instance uses is shown by GET /api/secure/source/relay-config.
#
# TWO distinct URLs, because they are reached by different parties:
#   YOURPHR_RELAY_URL        — where the BACKEND polls /pending. May be private/cluster-internal
#                              (e.g. http://yourphr-relay.yourphr.svc.cluster.local:8080).
#   YOURPHR_RELAY_PUBLIC_URL — the public origin the PROVIDER redirects the user's browser to.
#                              <this>/callback is the OAuth redirect_uri you register with each
#                              FHIR vendor; it must match EXACTLY.
# If you run a single publicly-reachable https relay, set only YOURPHR_RELAY_URL — the public URL
# defaults to it. Set YOURPHR_RELAY_PUBLIC_URL when the poll URL is internal or non-https.
# Unset both to use the project's dev/demo relay (https://relay.nerdsbythehour.com).
# YOURPHR_RELAY_URL=https://relay.example.org
# YOURPHR_RELAY_PUBLIC_URL=https://relay.example.org
# Shared secret gating /pending — must match the value configured on your relay.
# YOURPHR_RELAY_SECRET=
